Z||000003||artherd||07-15-2001||03:07 AM||||Velocity stacks with individual throttle bodies per cylender :)  (someday, first I just have to get the $ together to start the swap :)   <P>(how goes your tranny machineing?)<P>Best!<BR>Ben<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000004||Will||07-15-2001||09:09 AM||||I made cardboard templates of the profile of the starter, both horizontally and vertically. I used these to cut away the webbing using a hand operated mill and a 1/2" ball-end cutter. It took about 2-2&1/2 hours, but it fit right the first try.<P>The end of the throwout shaft does not need to be touched up on this particular engine ('95), but would for a '00 engine because of revisions to the coolant crossover manifold.<P>I think the best way to deal with the lower right bellhousing bolt is to put a bolt in the transmission, and notch the boss on the engine to clear it. This only requires a slight notch which can be made in about 30 seconds with a die grinder.<BR>I'm going to make up a clamp piece which will pick up both the bolt which goes into the transmission and a bolt going into the engine boss from the front of the boss.<p>[This message has been edited by Will (edited 07-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Jay||07-15-2001||06:22 PM||||Will, I'm actually using a Ractive intake made for a Honda civic.  It has the perfect shape to fit inside the drivers side wheel well right where the Holley scoop takes it's air from.  On the end I put the biggest K&N filter I could get.  The shape is perfect but I think I want to go with a larger diameter intake.  The Honda civic intake is much smaller than the throttle body opening.  I'll try to dig up a photo for you.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Jay||07-15-2001||06:27 PM||||This picture isn't the best but you get the idea.  It turns downward into the wheel well.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Northstar_engine_2000_2.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||terryk||07-16-2001||01:32 PM||||I heard 1:1 air flow to HP, but I think it's more like .9-.95. .95 gms/hp. There's some Finnegan's Finagle Factors not accounted for in that ratio.<P>I can say that my two 3800 SC Series I's flowed ~200 gm/s at 5500 RPM stock for 205HP rating and with the bigger blowers and pulley's I can hit 240+. My GTP seems to top out at around 250 gm/s the one time I checked.<P>Seems 1:1 is a good rule of thumb but not much else.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Will||07-16-2001||07:21 PM||||So I'm going to need close to 300 gm/s to keep my Northstar happy?<BR>Hmmmm...<P>What kind of MAF's do LS1's, LT1's, and LT4's use?<BR>The one on my dad's L99 powered Z only goes to 255 gm/s (I think), which is not really enough for a high powered engine.<P>My '95 Northstar electronics don't use a MAF, but I want to go to MAF electronics someday.||||reg||1
